<BaseLayout title="Eugenics and institutional legacy" description="Ronald Fisher’s eugenic commitments, their institutional setting, and the modern debate over commemoration." pagefind>
  <article class="page-shell narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Historical context / eugenics</p>
    <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">Eugenics was not incidental to Fisher’s career.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Fisher treated heredity, fertility, class, and national decline as connected scientific and political questions. His advocacy must be described directly rather than hidden behind a general statement that he was “a man of his time.”</p>

    <h2>What he supported</h2>
    <p>Fisher was a long-standing member of the Eugenics Society and promoted differential family allowances intended to encourage larger families among professional and upper social classes. His arguments relied on hereditary interpretations of social position and ability. He also defended eugenics after the Second World War, when Nazi racial policy had made the movement’s coercive and racist consequences impossible to treat as abstract.</p>

    <h2>Statistics, genetics, and authority</h2>
    <p>Eugenics shaped institutions in which early biometry and human genetics developed. Fisher’s 1933 appointment as Galton Professor of Eugenics was not merely a historical job title: it placed his statistical and genetic authority inside a program designed to influence policy about reproduction and population quality.</p>

    <h2>Race and disagreement</h2>
    <p>Fisher’s writings did not form a single unchanging doctrine, and historians disagree over how particular remarks should be classified. That complexity does not erase his active commitment to eugenics or the hierarchies built into it. The editorial task is to distinguish documented positions, later interpretation, and present-day moral judgment—not to use nuance as exoneration.</p>

    <h2>Commemoration</h2>
    <p>Universities and scientific societies have reconsidered buildings, lectures, prizes, and memorials bearing Fisher’s name. Renaming does not remove the work from history; it changes who an institution chooses to honor. This index retains eponyms where they remain useful for discovery while making attribution and context visible on the same page.</p>

    <h2>Limits of this account</h2>
    <p>This essay is an orientation, not a complete history of British eugenics, colonial science, or scientific racism. Contributions should be corrected when stronger archival evidence changes a claim, and quotations should be read in their full documentary context.</p>

